{"belief_id":"blf_ngram_capacity_curve","created_at":"2026-07-22T04:50:00-07:00","created_by":"claude-ledger","evidence_ids":["evd_run_ngram_capacity_128_discovery","evd_run_ngram_capacity_256_discovery"],"fingerprint":"d313abba9088c69e","limitations":["only 64/128/256 probed; 512x OOMs","2000-step scope; longer-budget transfer unknown","memorization/gap check deferred (per-step val gap too noisy)"],"next_test":"Matched-compute longer-budget transfer check of 256x (separate scope); optional memorization check via smoothed final train-loss from observable_curves.json.","rationale":"Two paired 3-seed discoveries on shared seeds map the curve; consistent with SCONE monotonic capacity scaling, with saturation approaching within feasible VRAM.","scope":{"data_split_sha256":"ed8ea0554010df9fbfa47746adc6643d6446797753eb377c0b35413bdcf2fca3","max_steps":2000,"outcome_id":"out_val_bpb","stop_mode":"steps"},"statement":"N-gram VE capacity scales val_bpb monotonically with diminishing returns at the 2000-step scope: 64->128x gives -0.00271, 64->256x gives -0.00405 (both paired-t<-18, clear the 2x floor), but the 128->256 increment (-0.00134) is itself sub-floor. 256x is the best FEASIBLE operating point (512x OOMs ~226GB); 128x is a cheaper option capturing 67% of the gain at half the VRAM.","status":"strongly_supported","subject_ids":["hyp_ngram_capacity_128","hyp_ngram_capacity_256","mech_ngram_short_context_memory","clm_r2_ngram_scone_scaling_embeddings_3"],"supersedes_belief_id":"","tags":["ngram","capacity","curve","explore"],"version":1}
