{"belief_id":"blf_capacity_saturating_and_broad","created_at":"2026-07-22T20:25:00-07:00","created_by":"claude-ledger","evidence_ids":["evd_run_ngram384_capacity","evd_obs_capacity_decile_decomposition"],"fingerprint":"3053465f0c4320a6","limitations":["3 seeds","proxy observable for the context-collision mechanism"],"next_test":"Product-key/learned sparse memory, justified as learned-vs-random routing rather than more capacity.","rationale":"Sub-threshold 384x + flat relative decile profile.","scope":{"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel n_layer=8","regime":"single-pass ~0.47 epoch 2000-step H200","scope_key":{"data_split_sha256":"ed8ea0554010df9fbfa47746adc6643d6446797753eb377c0b35413bdcf2fca3","max_steps":2000,"outcome_id":"out_val_bpb","stop_mode":"steps"}},"statement":"The n-gram hash-capacity axis is SATURATING and its mechanism is NOT rare-token-specific. (a) 384x beats 256x by only -0.000611 (t=-2.90, real but 55% of the signal-fraction prediction and below the -0.001 advance threshold); per-SF slope halved from -0.0013 to -0.00072. (b) The 64x->256x gain is broad-spectrum: RELATIVE val-loss improvement is flat-to-declining with rarity (5.26% frequent vs 4.14% rare) and flat across position. Together these WEAKEN the 'collision interference on rare n-grams' rationale for brute-force capacity scaling. IMPLICATION for the architecture campaign: more RANDOM hash capacity is played out; the case for the sparse-memory bet must rest on LEARNED retrieval (related contexts sharing a slot) being qualitatively better than random hashing -- not on simply buying more buckets.","status":"supported","subject_ids":["hyp_ngram384_capacity","mech_ngram_short_context_memory"],"supersedes_belief_id":"","tags":["capacity","saturating","causal","mechanism"],"version":1}
{"belief_id":"blf_diagnose_is_token_weighted_not_byte_weighted","created_at":"2026-07-22T21:00:00-07:00","created_by":"claude-observable","evidence_ids":["evd_obs_capacity_decile_decomposition"],"fingerprint":"ae216540c8d9b0e2","limitations":["does not tell us the true per-byte position profile"],"next_test":"Byte-weight the DIAGNOSE accumulators (_m = token byte-length instead of indicator) to make the decomposition commensurate with val_bpb; then re-run the seg position test.","rationale":"Implied bytes/token differ 3.3% on identical data -- arithmetically impossible unless weightings differ.","scope":{"model":"nanoGPT rsi/daniel n_layer=8","regime":"single-pass ~0.47 epoch 2000-step H200","scope_key":{"data_split_sha256":"ed8ea0554010df9fbfa47746adc6643d6446797753eb377c0b35413bdcf2fca3","max_steps":2000,"outcome_id":"out_val_bpb","stop_mode":"steps"}},"statement":"INSTRUMENTATION LIMIT: the DIAGNOSE decomposition reports mean nats per TOKEN (its mask `_m = (_tb[_y] > 0)` is a byte-presence INDICATOR, not a byte count), whereas the challenge metric val_bpb is per BYTE. The two weightings can move in OPPOSITE directions. Demonstrated: 256x seg-vs-no-seg gave val_bpb -0.001731 (seg BETTER) while DIAGNOSE per-token nats were +0.0801 (seg WORSE in all 8 position bins). Since the val set is identical, implied bytes/token would have to differ 4.05 vs 4.18 (3.3%), which is impossible -- proving the two measure different weightings. CONSEQUENCE: the seg-mask position-profile causal test CANNOT be run with DIAGNOSE as written, and any DIAGNOSE contrast whose two metrics disagree in sign must be discarded. The capacity contrast (64x vs 256x) is unaffected: there both metrics move the same way (nats -0.1323, bpb -0.003734), so that conclusion stands.","status":"strongly_supported","subject_ids":["mech_doc_boundary_attention"],"supersedes_belief_id":"","tags":["instrumentation","observable","invalid","doc_mask"],"version":1}
